%I A260335 #20 Sep 12 2026 21:32:14
%S A260335 79,223,359,439,443,499,659,727,839,1087,1091,1171,1223,1327,1367,
%T A260335 1523,1567,1627,1787,1811,1847,1907,1987,2027,2099,2143,2207,2251,
%U A260335 2399,2459,2467,2543,2659,2711,2971,3023,3163,3251,3391,3719,3739,3803,3967,4139,4159,4271
%N A260335 Prime determinants of forms with class number greater than 2.
%C A260335 Also primes p == 3 (mod 4) such that Z[sqrt(p)] = Z[x]/(x^2 - p) is not a unique factorization domain (or equivalently, not a principal ideal domain). - _Jianing Song_, Feb 17 2021
%C A260335 The class number being greater than 2 is significant because it indicates an even greater "failure" of unique factorization than in rings with class number 2. The classic example of a non-UFD, Z[sqrt(-5)], has class number 2. In that ring, we see that both factorizations of 6 have two nonunit factors. But in a domain with class number greater than 2, like Z[sqrt(79)], distinct factorizations can differ in the number of nonunit factors. For example, 78 = 2 * 3 * 13 = (-1)(1 - sqrt(79))(1 + sqrt(79)), with the latter factorization having one nonunit factor fewer than the former. - _Alonso del Arte_, Jul 16 2026
%H A260335 M. Suryanarayana, <a href="https://www.ias.ac.in/article/fulltext/seca/002/02/0178-0179">Positive determinants of binary quadratic forms whose class-number is 2</a>, Proceedings of the Indian Academy of Sciences. Section A, 2 (1935), 178-179.
%t A260335 Select[4Range[1250] - 1, PrimeQ[#] && NumberFieldClassNumber[Sqrt[#]] > 2 &] (* _Alonso del Arte_, Jul 16 2026 *)
%o A260335 (PARI) isA260335(p) = isprime(p) && (p%4==3) && quadclassunit(4*p)[1] > 1 \\ _Jianing Song_, Feb 17 2021
